  clock: 4  # cd71
  nonce: cd71  # cd71
  message: "PROMOTED P2 -> P1 on 2026-08-21, on explicit owner authorisation, recorded here because a re-rank that lives only in conversation is indistinguishable next session from drift.\n\nWHY: this item's own evidence was being cited on a different item. INV-vaduk was promoted P2 -> P1 on 2026-08-17 with a note justifying the rank by the 48-of-85-flows-at-22.9%-precision finding from docs/measurements/0001 consequence 4 -- the env_read -> host_secret vocabulary problem, which is THIS item's subject. INV-vaduk's statement is about primitives catalogued under MORE THAN ONE boundary; env_read rows are under exactly one. LIVE.md carried the same misattribution in its P1 line.\n\nINV-vaduk closed satisfied on 2026-08-21 (PR #463). What it actually fixed was a declaration gap in the multi-boundary catalogue -- real work, but not this lever. So on its closure the 48-of-85 lever became unowned at P1, and this promotion puts the rank on the item that holds the evidence.\n\nTHE GOVERNING RULE IS UNCHANGED: WI-sivuz's PRE-REGISTERED bands, named before any interval was computed. 0001 put taint precision at ~41%, which is the <50% band -- 'precision is the headline problem and recall work should stop until it is understood'. Under that band this is the largest single lever the measurement identifies.\n\nWHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and must be re-derived before anyone leans on it: I have NOT re-run 0001's adjudication or re-verified the 48/85 or 22.9% figures against the current tree. 0001's headline is known STALE -- its census drifted 60 -> 298 flows in nine days (docs/measurements/0003), which is why 0003 used a delta A/B instead of a re-run. So the RANK rests on the band ruling plus the qualitative finding (a boundary whose members are GOOS / Getwd / Executable / navigator.platform auto-deriving a label that means 'secret'), which is durable; the specific NUMBERS want re-measurement. Whoever takes this should measure first and treat 48/85 and 22.9% as provisional."  # cd71
- op: discuss  # 0f6b
  at: '2026-08-21T19:15:07Z'  # 0f6b
  by: agent  # 0f6b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0f6b
  clock: 5  # 0f6b
  nonce: 0f6b  # 0f6b
  message: "NUMBERS RE-MEASURED AS THE THREAD REQUIRED, AND THEY ARE STRONGER THAN THE ONES\nTHEY REPLACE. 2026-08-21, dev 09921c57a1.\n\nThis item's own promotion note says the 48-of-85 / 22.9% figures are provisional\nand must be re-derived before anyone leans on them, because 0001's census drifted\n60 -> 298 flows in nine days. I did NOT re-run 0001's adjudication. I measured the\nCATALOGUE instead, which is the durable half of the claim and is not subject to\ncensus drift at all: the question \"does env_read mean what host_secret means\" is\nanswerable from the rows, without any repo.\n\nTHE CENSUS. All 15 shipped io_primitives catalogues, every row whose boundary\nincludes env_read:\n\n    TOTAL env_read primitives: 195, across 13 languages\n      python 59 · javascript 31 · erlang 18 · elixir 16 · scala 16 · go 11\n      haskell 10 · objc 9 · java 7 · rust 7 · c 6 · swift 4 · bash 1\n\n    READS AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE            36   (18.5%)\n    READS HOST / PROCESS DESCRIPTION        159   (81.5%)\n\n(Hand-corrected from a keyword pass in both directions: rust std::env.var /\nvar_os / vars moved INTO the env-var bucket, haskell getArgs /\ngetExecutablePath / getProgName moved OUT of it. Net unchanged at 36.)\n\nSo FOUR FIFTHS of the boundary that auto-derives the taint label `host_secret`\ndoes not read a secret in any sense. A sample of what is currently labelled\nhost_secret, all verbatim rows:\n\n    java.lang.Runtime.freeMemory / maxMemory / totalMemory / availableProcessors\n    javascript os.cpus / os.totalmem / os.freemem / os.arch / os.release\n    javascript navigator.language / navigator.onLine / navigator.cookieEnabled\n    python platform.python_version / platform.machine / shutil.get_terminal_size\n    python os.getcwd / pathlib.Path.cwd / pathlib.Path.home\n    go runtime.GOOS / runtime.GOARCH / os.Getwd / os.Executable\n    erlang os.perf_counter / os.system_time / os.version\n\n`Runtime.freeMemory` is not a secret. Neither is `platform.python_version`.\n\n*** AND THE CATALOGUES ALREADY CONTRADICT EACH OTHER ABOUT IT, IN WRITING. ***\nThis is the finding I did not expect and it decides the fix shape. python.yaml\nline 574 REFUSES to row process-state primitives, and states this item's thesis\nas its reason:\n\n    \"Deliberately NOT getpid / cpu_count / times: env_read rows auto-derive\n     host_secret TAINT SOURCES (AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP), and a pid is not a\n     secret - rowing it would manufacture false sources.\"\n\nTwo other catalogues row exactly that primitive anyway:\n\n    c.yaml:109       functions: [getcwd, gethostname, getuid, getgid, getpid]\n    elixir.yaml:238  functions: [get_env, fetch_env, fetch_env!, get_pid, ...]\n\nThree catalogues, one question, two opposite answers, and the correct reasoning\nwritten down in prose in the one that got it right with NOTHING enforcing it on\nthe other two. That is the one-fact-N-private-copies shape this project hit on\nthree separate axes in the previous session (edge_type / Symbol.kind /\nio_boundary), and the remedy there was always the same: ONE registry-backed\npredicate plus a GATE that fails CI on the next copy — never a better literal,\nand never a per-row edit.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE. It settles that the mismatch is real, sizes\nit at 159 of 195 rows, and shows the project already knows the answer in one\nplace and not the others. It does NOT re-derive 48/85 or 22.9%, and those should\nstill be treated as provisional; nothing here depends on them.\n\nTHE DECISION IS STILL A DECISION, and it is the owner's because it changes what\nevery shipped host_secret claim MEANS. The two options this item names are not\nequal in safety, and that is worth stating before anyone picks:\n\n  (1) SPLIT the boundary — env_read (36 rows, env-var reads) vs a new\n      host-description boundary (159 rows), with AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP deriving\n      host_secret from the former only. Direction: removes ~81% of auto-derived\n      host_secret sources while KEEPING every real one. Precision-positive,\n      and it loses no true source.\n  (2) STOP deriving a security-loaded label from the boundary at all. Direction:\n      removes ALL host_secret sources, so every host_secret claim becomes\n      vacuously confirmable. That is the DELETING direction, and it turns a\n      claim that used to say something into one that cannot fail. I would not\n      ship it without an explicit ruling.\n\nEither way the row-level judgement (does THIS primitive read a secret) must end\nup in ONE place with a gate, or the c.yaml / elixir.yaml / python.yaml\ndisagreement simply reappears at the next catalogue.\n\nNOT MEASURED: how many FLOWS on a corpus each option would remove — the census\ncounts catalogue rows, not call sites, and a row with no call sites costs\nnothing. That A/B is the honest next step once the vocabulary question is\nanswered, and per this project's own rule it must be read on FINDINGS, both signs."  # 0f6b
- op: discuss  # 0c1a
  at: '2026-08-21T19:38:11Z'  # 0c1a
  by: agent  # 0c1a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0c1a
  clock: 6  # 0c1a
  nonce: 0c1a  # 0c1a
  message: "INVESTIGATED AGAINST THE ADRs, THE SPEC, THE COMMIT HISTORY AND THE ADJACENT\nRULINGS, ON OWNER INSTRUCTION, 2026-08-21. The answer is NOT a new vocabulary\nquestion. It is a case ruling under law this project has already written down\nand already applied TWICE, and the catalogue was populated in violation of it.\n\n*** 1. THE BOUNDARY'S MEANING IS ALREADY DECLARED, AND 159 ROWS DO NOT MATCH IT. ***\nADR-0016's controlled vocabulary table:\n\n    | env_read | inbound | Read from process environment |\n    | os.environ, std::env::var(), command-line args |\n\n\"Read from process environment\", with command-line args explicitly in scope. By\nthat definition the 36 env-var rows plus the argv rows are ON-axis and the label\nthey derive is exactly what the ADR intends. runtime.GOOS, os.Getwd,\njava.lang.Runtime.freeMemory, platform.python_version, navigator.onLine,\nshutil.get_terminal_size, os.uname, pwd.getpwuid and their ~150 siblings are not\nreads from the process environment by any reading of that sentence. So this item\ndoes not need a new axiom -- it needs the existing one enforced. Under the\nfundamental-concept audit's own trichotomy that makes it a CASE RULING UNDER\nEXISTING LAW (sort values against a declared axiom), not a NEW PRINCIPLE\n(declare an axis). The cheaper half of the audit, not the expensive one.\n\n*** 2. THE ADR ALREADY ANSWERS THE \"SO WHAT DO WE DO WITH THEM\" QUESTION. ***\nADR-0016 section Risk classification, verbatim:\n\n    \"every read-side *sensitive* boundary (env_read, net_recv, ipc_recv,\n     db_read) is an untrusted source; fs_read and browser_storage_read are\n     deliberately quiet (SENSITIVITY DEPENDS ON WHAT IS STORED).\"\n\nThat is precisely the property of the 159 rows. A host-description read is\nsensitive or not depending on what the host is and who is asking -- which is the\nstated reason fs_read is quiet. The ADR did not fail to anticipate this case; it\nalready carved out the category and named the treatment.\n\n*** 3. AND THE PROJECT HAS APPLIED THAT TREATMENT TWICE, WITH THE ESCAPE HATCH\nALREADY SHIPPED. *** io_boundary.py, on the browser-storage pair:\n\n    \"The read side (browser_storage_read) is intentionally NOT in\n     AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP -- MATCHING HOW fs_read IS TREATED -- because the\n     sensitivity of a browser storage read depends on what's stored\n     (project-local catalogs can add taint_sources entries for their threat\n     model).\"\n\nfs_read: quiet. browser_storage_read: quiet, explicitly by analogy. A\nhost-description boundary would be the THIRD application of one rule, not a new\none, and the mechanism for users who DO care about host fingerprinting already\nexists and is already documented -- project-local taint_sources, the same\noverlay path INV-fotav shipped.\n\n*** 4. HOW THE ROWS GOT IN, AND WHY IT IS THE FAMILIAR SHAPE. *** The widening is\nmostly one commit: 12133e4ad0 (2026-08-15), \"the stdlib climb -- descriptor-level\nI/O rowed, 47 dated audits, stdlib blockers to ZERO\". Its driver was COMPLETENESS\n(the self-proof's blocker list, 89 modules -> 43), and env_read was the nearest\nbucket for \"reads something about the host\". That same commit's message says:\n\n    \"FOUR REFUSALS ARE PART OF THE DESIGN, each measured, none hypothetical:\n      - getpid/cpu_count/times are NOT env_read rows: env_read auto-derives\n        host_secret taint sources, AND A PID IS NOT A SECRET.\"\n\nSo the correct test was known, stated, and applied to three primitives -- in the\nSAME COMMIT that rowed platform.platform, platform.architecture,\nplatform.libc_ver, platform.mac_ver, platform.freedesktop_os_release, os.uname,\nos.getcwd, os.getlogin, os.sysconf, os.confstr, shutil.get_terminal_size, and the\nwhole pwd/grp surface, every one of which fails that test identically. A terminal\nsize is not a secret either.\n\nThat is the shape this project keeps meeting: ONE FACT (is this primitive a\nsecret?), living in PROSE in one catalogue's notes field, applied BY HAND at every\nrow, drifting inside a single commit and then across catalogues -- c.yaml:109 and\nelixir.yaml:238 row getpid/get_pid outright, contradicting python.yaml's written\nrefusal. It is the edge_type / Symbol.kind / io_boundary pattern again, and the\nremedy is the one that worked all three times: ONE REGISTRY-BACKED PREDICATE PLUS\nA GATE, never a better literal and never a per-row edit.\n\n*** 5. RECOMMENDATION, in precedent order rather than preference order. ***\n  (a) SPLIT, on ADR-0016's own definition: env_read keeps reads from the process\n      environment (env vars + argv, ~36-46 rows); a new inbound boundary --\n      host_info_read or similar -- takes the ~150 host/process-description rows.\n      This is an ADDITIVE vocabulary change (KNOWN_IO_BOUNDARIES grows; existing\n      claims keep validating), and the user-visible break is exactly the intended\n      correction: `must_not_exist: env_read` stops meaning \"reads anything about\n      the machine\".\n  (b) THE NEW BOUNDARY IS QUIET -- NOT in AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP -- for the reason\n      ADR-0016 already gives for fs_read and io_boundary.py already gives for\n      browser_storage_read. Users who care add project-local taint_sources.\n  (c) A GATE, or this returns. The predicate belongs in one place with CI behind\n      it; the getpid disagreement across three catalogues is what a rule enforced\n      only by a notes field looks like after four months.\n  (d) NOT option 2 from the filing (drop env_read from AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP\n      entirely). It deletes the 36 rows that ARE what the ADR means, making every\n      host_secret claim vacuously confirmable -- a claim that cannot fail. The\n      ADR classes env_read as sensitive deliberately and that classification is\n      correct for env vars.\n\n*** 6. ADJACENT SWEEP (audit Step 5), flagged not resolved. *** This item already\nnames the sibling: INV-vaduk shape 4 asks whether `logging` is a BOUNDARY or a\nPURPOSE (haskell hPutStrLn catalogued as both fs_write and logging, deriving two\nsinks in two zones from one write). `logging` and `external_potential` are both in\nCATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES while answering a different question from \"what kind of I/O\ncrossing is this\" -- the same suspicion, one field over. Worth its own pass; not\nopened here.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED. *** No FLOW-level measurement: the 195/159/36 census\ncounts catalogue rows, and a row with no call sites costs nothing. How many\nfindings the split removes on a corpus is the A/B that should precede the merge,\nread on findings in both directions per the standing rule. I also have not\nenumerated which of the 159 are genuine syscalls (os.uname, gethostname, getcwd)\nversus in-process constants that are not I/O at all (runtime.GOOS, sys.platform,\nplatform.python_version) -- that sub-split may matter for whether the new boundary\nis one bucket or two, and it is a real question rather than a tidy-up."  # 0c1a
- op: discuss  # 0bc9
  at: '2026-08-25T04:14:00Z'  # 0bc9
  by: agent  # 0bc9
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0bc9
  clock: 7  # 0bc9
  nonce: 0bc9  # 0bc9
  message: "PHASE 0 RE-VERIFICATION AT TIP, 2026-08-25, dev e41a655d6d. CONFIRMED, AND\nLARGER THAN FILED.\n\nAUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP (taint.py:1268) still reads env_read -> host_secret, and\ngo.yaml's env_read block still carries os.Getwd / os.Executable / os.Hostname /\nos.UserHomeDir / os.UserCacheDir / os.UserConfigDir plus runtime.GOOS /\nruntime.GOARCH.\n\nTHE POPULATION IS NOT A GO PROBLEM. Full census of the shipped catalogues:\n195 env_read entries across 15 languages. Beyond the go rows this item names:\n\n  javascript  navigator.userAgent/language/platform/geolocation/cookieEnabled/\n              onLine, window.location/navigator/screen/document,\n              os.cpus/totalmem/freemem/homedir/tmpdir/release/type/arch,\n              process.platform/arch/version/cwd     (and document.cookie, which\n              IS credential-bearing -- the same boundary holds both extremes)\n  python      15 rows: platform.* (7), locale.* (3), pathlib.Path.cwd/home/\n              expanduser/absolute, os.path.expanduser/expandvars, posixpath.*,\n              shutil.get_terminal_size, tempfile.gettempdir/gettempprefix,\n              sys.platform/version/executable/path, os.uname/getcwd/getcwdb,\n              pwd.*/grp.* (6), AND os.getrandom -- which is a CSPRNG read, not\n              an environment read in any sense\n  java        Runtime.availableProcessors/totalMemory/freeMemory/maxMemory\n  objc        NSProcessInfo.processName/hostName/operatingSystemVersionString/\n              physicalMemory/processorCount, NSBundle.*\n  haskell     System.Info.os/arch/compilerName/compilerVersion\n  erlang      os.type/version/system_time/perf_counter\n  scala       Properties.javaHome/tmpDir/userDir/userHome/userName/scalaHome\n  rust        std::env.consts, current_exe\n  c           unistd.getcwd/gethostname/getuid/getgid/getpid\n\nCensus file: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/campaign_p0_08252026/env_read_census.txt\n\nSo the 48/85 flows at 22.9% precision measured for go is a lower bound on the\nlabel's blast radius, not the whole of it. Phase 2 of the campaign; the\nconcept audit runs before any refactor and the resulting vocabulary is an\nOWNER RATIFICATION point."  # 0bc9
